//? Title: Compare survival curves with log-rank and Fleming–Harrington tests
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Choose where in follow-up a survival test should look for a difference

The log-rank test compares groups one event time at a time. At each distinct event time \(t\), the \(d\) deaths are shared among the \(n\) subjects at risk, and a group with \(n_g\) of them is expected to contribute \(d\,n_g/n\) deaths if all groups share one hazard. Summing observed minus expected deaths over event times gives a vector \(O-E\). Its hypergeometric covariance \(V\) gives the chi-square statistic \((O-E)^\top V^{-}(O-E)\) on \(G-1\) degrees of freedom. Strata are handled by forming risk sets within each stratum and summing \(O-E\) and \(V\) across strata.

Every event time counts equally in the log-rank sum, which makes it the most powerful rank test when hazards are proportional. Harrington and Fleming weight each time by \(w(t)=\hat S(t-)^\rho\{1-\hat S(t-)\}^\gamma\), where \(\hat S\) is the pooled Kaplan–Meier estimate just before \(t\):
- \(G(0,0)\) is the log-rank test.
- \(G(1,0)\) is the Peto–Peto–Prentice generalized Wilcoxon test, which emphasizes early differences.
- \(G(0,1)\) emphasizes late differences, such as a treatment whose effect is delayed.
- \(G(1,1)\) emphasizes the middle of follow-up.

The example compares the size and power of these four weights in three two-arm trials of 150 subjects per arm, with control hazard 1 and administrative censoring at time 2. The treatment arm has a hazard ratio of 0.6 throughout, of 0.4 until time 0.5 and 1 afterwards (early effect), or of 1 until 0.5 and 0.4 afterwards (delayed effect).

```{cpp, id=logrank, file="figures/logrank_power.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/survival_tests.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::SurvivalGroupTestOptions;
using dm::stats::SurvivalObservation;

// Piecewise-exponential lifetime: hazard h1 before time c, h2 afterwards.
double lifetime(double h1, double h2, double c, std::mt19937_64& random) {
    const double e = std::exponential_distribution<double>(1)(random);
    return e < h1 * c ? e / h1 : c + (e - h1 * c) / h2;
}

struct Scenario {
    std::string name;
    double early_ratio, late_ratio;
};

struct Trial {
    std::vector<SurvivalObservation> records;
    std::vector<int> arm;
};

Trial simulate(std::size_t per_arm, const Scenario& s, std::mt19937_64& random) {
    Trial trial;
    for (int arm = 0; arm < 2; ++arm)
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < per_arm; ++i) {
            const double t = arm == 0 ? lifetime(1, 1, .5, random)
                                      : lifetime(s.early_ratio, s.late_ratio, .5, random);
            trial.records.push_back({std::min(t, 2.0), t <= 2.0});
            trial.arm.push_back(arm);
        }
    return trial;
}

const std::array<SurvivalGroupTestOptions, 4> weights{
    SurvivalGroupTestOptions{}, {.rho = 1}, {.gamma = 1}, {.rho = 1, .gamma = 1}};

std::array<double, 4> rejection(std::size_t per_arm, const Scenario& s, int reps,
                                std::mt19937_64& random) {
    std::array<double, 4> rate{};
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
        const auto trial = simulate(per_arm, s, random);
        for (std::size_t k = 0; k < weights.size(); ++k)
            rate[k] += dm::stats::survival_group_test(trial.records, trial.arm, weights[k])
                           .p_value < .05;
    }
    for (double& v : rate)
        v /= reps;
    return rate;
}

int main() {
    std::mt19937_64 random(2024);
    const std::vector<Scenario> scenarios{{"No difference (size)", 1, 1},
                                          {"Proportional, HR 0.6", .6, .6},
                                          {"Early effect", .4, 1},
                                          {"Delayed effect", 1, .4}};
    std::println("| Scenario | Log-rank G(0,0) | Peto G(1,0) | Late G(0,1) | Middle G(1,1) |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& s : scenarios) {
        const auto rate = rejection(150, s, 2000, random);
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", s.name, rate[0], rate[1],
                     rate[2], rate[3]);
    }

    // Power against sample size under the delayed effect.
    std::vector<double> sizes;
    std::array<std::vector<double>, 4> power;
    for (std::size_t n = 25; n <= 300; n += 25) {
        const auto rate = rejection(n, scenarios[3], 1000, random);
        sizes.push_back(double(n));
        for (std::size_t k = 0; k < 4; ++k)
            power[k].push_back(rate[k]);
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(sizes, power[0], "Log-rank G(0,0)", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .line(sizes, power[1], "Peto G(1,0)", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .line(sizes, power[2], "Late G(0,1)", {46, 139, 87}, 2)
        .line(sizes, power[3], "Middle G(1,1)", {120, 81, 169}, 2)
        .title("Power under a delayed treatment effect")
        .x_label("Subjects per arm")
        .y_label("Rejection rate at 5%")
        .ylim(0, 1.3)
        .size(700, 400);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/logrank_power.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Scenario | Log-rank G(0,0) | Peto G(1,0) | Late G(0,1) | Middle G(1,1) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| No difference (size) | 0.052 | 0.049 | 0.057 | 0.045 |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 0.980 | 0.964 | 0.931 | 0.960 |
| Early effect | 0.669 | 0.909 | 0.113 | 0.289 |
| Delayed effect | 0.905 | 0.560 | 0.991 | 0.959 |
\image(figures/logrank_power.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Power of four Fleming–Harrington weights at the 5% level under a delayed treatment effect (hazard ratio 1 until time 0.5, then 0.4), by subjects per arm. 1000 trials per point.)
\alttext(Four rising curves. The green late-weighted G(0,1) curve is highest, followed closely by the purple G(1,1) curve and the blue log-rank curve. The orange Peto G(1,0) curve is clearly lowest throughout and is still well below 1 at 300 subjects per arm.)

All four weights hold the 5% level when the arms do not differ (4.5–5.7% over 2000 trials, within simulation error). Under proportional hazards the log-rank test is the most powerful, as theory says, but the other weights lose only a few points. The non-proportional scenarios are different. With an early effect, the Peto weight reaches 91% power against 67% for the log-rank, and the late weight is almost useless at 11%. With a delayed effect the ordering reverses: the late weight reaches 99%, the log-rank 91% and the Peto weight 56%.

A weight tuned to the wrong part of follow-up can lose most of the power, while the log-rank test is never the worst choice. That asymmetry, and the need to fix the weight before seeing the data, is why trials prespecify the log-rank test unless there is a mechanistic reason to expect a delayed effect, as with some immunotherapies. Maximum-combination tests that take the best of several weights with a multiplicity correction are a compromise.

Observed and expected counts, their covariance and the chi-square match R's `survdiff` for three groups with \(\rho\in\{0,0.5,1\}\) and with strata. \(\gamma>0\), which `survdiff` does not offer, is checked against direct two-group sums.

[Harrington and Fleming (1982)|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/69.3.553] · [Mantel (1966)|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5910392/]
